1
00:00:00,467 --> 00:00:02,567
15 November 1977. Bombay.

2
00:00:02,601 --> 00:00:07,600
A textile company comes to the public for the
first time.

3
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:13,033
Price of a share: ₹10. Total issue: ₹2.82
crore.

4
00:00:13,067 --> 00:00:18,433
About 58,000 people fill in the form. The
issue is oversubscribed seven times.

5
00:00:18,467 --> 00:00:21,033
The company's name: Reliance.

6
00:00:21,067 --> 00:00:28,567
49 years later. October 2026. A company from
the same group: Jio Platforms.

7
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:35,100
Issue size: ₹30,213 crore. Not on paper forms,
but on a phone, through UPI.

8
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:39,767
The money changed. The technology changed. But
the road is the same.

9
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:43,635
Every IPO, whether it's Jio or a small company
in your city,

10
00:00:43,668 --> 00:00:45,800
goes through this same journey.

11
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:50,833
Today, that whole journey: from an idea to the
listing bell.

12
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:55,367
And along the way, the questions you should
ask before every application.

13
00:00:57,567 --> 00:01:00,133
Let's start with a tea stall.

14
00:01:00,167 --> 00:01:05,000
Raju's Chai. One stall, then five, then fifty.

15
00:01:05,033 --> 00:01:10,433
Now Raju wants to open 500 stalls. He needs
about ₹100 crore.

16
00:01:10,467 --> 00:01:13,133
Where does the money come from? Three ways.

17
00:01:13,167 --> 00:01:17,767
One: your own savings. But nobody has savings
that big.

18
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:18,974
Two: a bank loan.

19
00:01:19,008 --> 00:01:23,900
But the interest is due every month, whether
the chai sells or not.

20
00:01:23,933 --> 00:01:25,340
Three: find a partner.

21
00:01:25,373 --> 00:01:29,433
They put in money and get a share of the
company in return.

22
00:01:29,833 --> 00:01:33,767
Raju picks partners. First, family and
friends.

23
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:39,367
Then bigger investors: angel investors,
venture capital, private equity funds.

24
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,567
Every partner gets shares. Small pieces of the
company.

25
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:48,467
But all of this is private. Nobody can buy or
sell these shares in a market.

26
00:01:48,867 --> 00:01:51,233
Then one day, two things happen.

27
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:55,600
The company needs so much money that private
partners aren't enough.

28
00:01:55,633 --> 00:01:59,767
And the old partners say: we want to sell our
stake and exit.

29
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:03,867
So the company says to the whole country:
come, be a partner.

30
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:07,113
That's called an IPO. Initial Public Offering.

31
00:02:07,146 --> 00:02:10,633
Shares offered to the public for the first
time.

32
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:13,367
IPO money can go to two places.

33
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:15,587
Fresh issue: new shares are created.

34
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:18,467
The money goes to the company, for its work.

35
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:22,031
Offer for sale: old partners sell their
shares.

36
00:02:22,064 --> 00:02:24,700
The money goes into their pocket.

37
00:02:24,733 --> 00:02:28,957
Jio's IPO was entirely a fresh issue: 27 crore
new shares.

38
00:02:28,990 --> 00:02:32,567
And a large part of it was for repaying loans.

39
00:02:34,767 --> 00:02:38,800
But bringing an IPO isn't as easy as putting
up a sign on a shop.

40
00:02:38,833 --> 00:02:44,467
The company hires merchant bankers. Their job:
run the whole IPO.

41
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:50,033
Along with lawyers, auditors and a registrar.
Months of preparation.

42
00:02:50,433 --> 00:02:53,600
The biggest task: one thick document.

43
00:02:53,633 --> 00:02:58,500
It's called the DRHP. Draft Red Herring
Prospectus.

44
00:02:58,533 --> 00:03:04,038
Why 'red herring'? The cover of such a
document used to carry a warning in red:

45
00:03:04,071 --> 00:03:05,467
this is not final.

46
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:08,733
Because it doesn't have the price or the dates
yet.

47
00:03:08,767 --> 00:03:10,324
Everything else is there.

48
00:03:10,358 --> 00:03:14,467
What the company does, how much it earns, how
much debt it has.

49
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:19,400
Who the promoters are. Which court cases are
running. And what the risks are.

50
00:03:19,433 --> 00:03:24,600
And the most useful section: objects of the
issue. Where the money will go.

51
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,533
This DRHP goes to SEBI. The stock market
regulator.

52
00:03:29,567 --> 00:03:33,800
And you can read it too. On SEBI's website,
completely free.

53
00:03:33,833 --> 00:03:39,867
SEBI asks questions. The company answers. Then
SEBI gives its observations.

54
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:42,967
Now let's clear up a big misunderstanding.

55
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,900
SEBI doesn't say whether a company is good or
bad.

56
00:03:46,933 --> 00:03:51,767
SEBI checks whether the important facts are
written down clearly.

57
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:58,067
So, 'SEBI approved it, so it's safe'? Wrong.
The decision is yours.

58
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:03,267
After the observations, the company has one
year to open the IPO.

59
00:04:05,467 --> 00:04:07,700
Now the question: what is a share worth?

60
00:04:07,733 --> 00:04:14,100
The bankers meet big investors to gauge
demand. This is called a roadshow.

61
00:04:14,133 --> 00:04:18,667
Then comes the RHP. Now with dates, and a
price band.

62
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:25,467
A price band means a range. Jio's: ₹1,065 to
₹1,119.

63
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:30,500
People bid within this range. That's why it's
called book building.

64
00:04:30,533 --> 00:04:33,776
Small investors often choose 'cut-off'.
Meaning:

65
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:36,467
whatever the final price is, I'm in.

66
00:04:36,867 --> 00:04:40,033
And shares come in lots, not one by one.

67
00:04:40,067 --> 00:04:45,133
By the rules, one lot is worth roughly ₹10,000
to ₹15,000.

68
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:51,133
Jio's lot: 12 shares. ₹13,428 at the upper
band.

69
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:56,700
A day before the issue opens, the big players
arrive: anchor investors.

70
00:04:56,733 --> 00:05:00,800
Mutual funds, insurance companies, foreign
funds.

71
00:05:00,833 --> 00:05:06,633
In Jio's IPO, 212 anchors together put in
₹8,110 crore.

72
00:05:06,667 --> 00:05:11,667
Their job: to show confidence. That big
investors are ready at this price.

73
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:13,105
But there's a condition.

74
00:05:13,139 --> 00:05:16,342
Half of the anchors' shares can't be sold for
30 days,

75
00:05:16,375 --> 00:05:18,367
and the other half for 90 days.

76
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:20,967
So they don't run away on listing day itself.

77
00:05:21,367 --> 00:05:25,100
Now the rest of the shares are divided. Think
of it as a cake.

78
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:30,333
50% for big institutions. They're called QIBs.

79
00:05:30,367 --> 00:05:36,867
15% for large investors who put in more than
₹2 lakh. NII.

80
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:42,933
And 35% for you. Retail investors, who put in
up to ₹2 lakh.

81
00:05:42,967 --> 00:05:46,094
This split is for profitable companies.

82
00:05:46,127 --> 00:05:51,333
In a company that can't show profits yet,
retail gets only 10%.

83
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:55,833
Because the risk there is higher. The rule
itself is protecting you.

84
00:05:58,033 --> 00:06:03,967
Now comes the big day. The issue opens, for at
least three working days.

85
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,933
Jio's: 21 to 23 October.

86
00:06:07,333 --> 00:06:11,633
In grandpa's time? Go to the bank, fill in a
form, attach a cheque.

87
00:06:11,667 --> 00:06:15,356
If you got shares, a paper certificate came
home.

88
00:06:15,389 --> 00:06:18,300
If not, a refund cheque, weeks later.

89
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:23,967
After Reliance's 1977 IPO, listing took about
two months.

90
00:06:24,367 --> 00:06:29,067
Today? Open a broker app, place a bid, approve
the mandate on UPI.

91
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:33,680
And here's something many people don't know:
the money isn't deducted.

92
00:06:33,714 --> 00:06:35,033
It's only blocked.

93
00:06:35,067 --> 00:06:40,081
This system is called ASBA. The money stays in
your account.

94
00:06:40,114 --> 00:06:42,267
You just can't spend it.

95
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:47,100
If you get shares, the money is deducted. If
not, the block is released.

96
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:48,393
For three days,

97
00:06:48,426 --> 00:06:52,633
the exchange shows live how much demand there
is in each category.

98
00:06:52,667 --> 00:06:58,767
This is called subscription. How many times
the bids exceed the shares on offer.

99
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:08,567
Jio: about 22 times overall. QIB more than 70
times. Retail about 2.7 times.

100
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:12,567
In other words, the big investors' queue was
far longer than yours.

101
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:19,352
In these days another market also runs,
without any rules: the grey market.

102
00:07:19,386 --> 00:07:20,967
Its number: GMP.

103
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,033
GMP is unofficial. There's a separate full
video on it.

104
00:07:28,233 --> 00:07:33,533
Three days are over. This day is called T day.
Now the clock starts.

105
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:40,033
SEBI's rule: T+3. Listing within three working
days of the issue closing.

106
00:07:40,433 --> 00:07:45,233
First step, T+1: allotment. Who gets the
shares?

107
00:07:45,267 --> 00:07:47,959
When retail demand is higher, the rule is:

108
00:07:47,992 --> 00:07:51,467
give at least one lot to as many people as
possible.

109
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:53,173
That's why there's a lottery.

110
00:07:53,207 --> 00:07:56,400
By computer, under the stock exchange's
supervision.

111
00:07:56,433 --> 00:07:57,957
And here's the twist:

112
00:07:57,990 --> 00:08:03,800
whether you applied for 1 lot or 14, you have
just one ticket in the lottery.

113
00:08:03,833 --> 00:08:09,331
Jio had about 8.5 crore shares for retail.
Lots of 12.

114
00:08:09,365 --> 00:08:13,800
So at most about 70 lakh people could win.

115
00:08:13,833 --> 00:08:17,265
If more people apply than that, not everyone
will get shares.

116
00:08:17,298 --> 00:08:19,500
No matter how much money they put in.

117
00:08:19,533 --> 00:08:24,436
Want to check your status? On the registrar's
site, or on NSE or BSE.

118
00:08:24,469 --> 00:08:26,100
Just enter your PAN.

119
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:29,802
T+2: if you got shares, they're in your demat.

120
00:08:29,835 --> 00:08:32,400
If not, the UPI block is released.

121
00:08:32,433 --> 00:08:36,212
Today India has more than 20 crore demat
accounts.

122
00:08:36,246 --> 00:08:39,267
The paper certificate is now history.

123
00:08:41,467 --> 00:08:46,900
T+3: listing day. But the share doesn't jump
straight into the market.

124
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:49,180
First, a special pre-open session.

125
00:08:49,213 --> 00:08:52,967
From 9 to 9:45 in the morning, only orders are
placed.

126
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:58,400
Then the exchange finds the price at which the
most shares can trade.

127
00:08:58,433 --> 00:09:03,067
That's the listing price. And normal trading
starts at 10.

128
00:09:03,467 --> 00:09:09,467
And yes, a bell rings too. Promoters, bankers,
cameras. A big celebration.

129
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:15,633
But the gap between the IPO price and the
listing price is your first profit or loss.

130
00:09:15,667 --> 00:09:22,700
Bajaj Housing Finance: IPO ₹70, listing ₹150.
More than double.

131
00:09:22,733 --> 00:09:29,867
Hyundai Motor India: IPO ₹1,960, listing
₹1,934. Lower.

132
00:09:29,900 --> 00:09:34,433
LIC: about 8% lower at listing.

133
00:09:34,467 --> 00:09:39,933
So a big name is no guarantee. Listing is just
one day's mood.

134
00:09:42,133 --> 00:09:46,867
The story doesn't end after listing. Now some
locks open.

135
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:50,439
After 30 days, half of the anchors' shares are
free.

136
00:09:50,472 --> 00:09:52,667
After 90 days, the other half.

137
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:55,900
Shares of the old private investors: after 6
months.

138
00:09:55,933 --> 00:10:01,406
And the promoters? At least 20% of their stake
is locked for 18 months.

139
00:10:01,439 --> 00:10:03,667
In some cases, three years.

140
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:06,974
When the locks open, there can be more
sellers.

141
00:10:07,007 --> 00:10:10,233
These dates are already written in the RHP.

142
00:10:10,633 --> 00:10:14,500
And the company's life changes. Now it's
public.

143
00:10:14,533 --> 00:10:15,965
Results every three months.

144
00:10:15,998 --> 00:10:19,333
Every major development has to be reported to
the exchange.

145
00:10:19,367 --> 00:10:25,233
And you? Now you're an owner. A small one, but
an owner. With voting rights too.

146
00:10:27,433 --> 00:10:32,000
So when the next IPO comes, ask these five
questions before you apply.

147
00:10:32,033 --> 00:10:38,700
One: who is the money going to? The company,
or the old partners' pockets?

148
00:10:38,733 --> 00:10:43,858
Two: what will the money be used for? A new
business, or an old loan?

149
00:10:43,891 --> 00:10:46,267
Read the objects of the issue.

150
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:51,933
Three: does the company earn? Is the profit
growing, or the debt?

151
00:10:51,967 --> 00:10:57,075
Four: is the price right? Check 'basis for
offer price' in the RHP.

152
00:10:57,108 --> 00:10:59,467
Compare with other companies.

153
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:05,533
Five: why am I applying? For one day of
listing, or for five years?

154
00:11:07,733 --> 00:11:10,167
One more time, the whole journey.

155
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:14,967
Partners needed. Bankers. DRHP. SEBI's
questions.

156
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,400
Price band. Anchors. Three days of bidding.

157
00:11:18,433 --> 00:11:24,967
T+1 lottery. T+2 demat. T+3 bell.

158
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,700
The road was the same in 1977. And it'll be
the same for the next IPO too.

159
00:11:30,733 --> 00:11:35,533
An IPO isn't a lottery ticket. It's a stake in
a company.

160
00:11:35,933 --> 00:11:41,058
Whoever at home is applying for an IPO for the
first time, son, dad or grandma,

161
00:11:41,091 --> 00:11:42,400
send this to them.

162
00:11:42,433 --> 00:11:46,633
This video is only for explanation. Not
investment advice.

163
00:11:46,667 --> 00:11:50,033
Understand money. This is Money Theory.

